================================================================================
Prior art and attribution
================================================================================

AgentController did not start from a blank page. Two existing projects shaped
it, in two different ways, and both are credited below.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Maestro (mobile.dev) — conceptual, no code
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    https://github.com/mobile-dev-inc/maestro
    Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0

Maestro established the interaction model that AgentController adopts: a
declarative, tolerant automation vocabulary in which a step waits for the UI to
settle instead of failing on the first miss. Two ideas in particular are taken
directly from Maestro's design:

  * Implicit find-and-retry on every interaction, so a control that renders a
    frame late self-heals rather than reporting a phantom failure.
  * Reusable, replayable "flows" as the unit of automation, rather than
    one-shot imperative commands.

NO MAESTRO SOURCE CODE IS INCLUDED IN THIS PROJECT. Maestro targets mobile
platforms on the JVM; AgentController's macOS (Swift), Windows (C#/.NET), and
iOS (TypeScript) backends are independent implementations written against
native platform APIs. The debt is one of design, not of code, and no Apache-2.0
obligation to Maestro attaches to this distribution.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. blitzdotdev/iPhone-mcp — code, vendored and extended
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    https://github.com/blitzdotdev/iPhone-mcp
    Copyright (c) 2025 Blitz
    Licensed under the MIT License

The iOS backend in `iOS/` began as a vendored copy of iPhone-mcp and retains
code derived from it. The upstream MIT license and copyright notice are
retained verbatim at `iOS/LICENSE`, and that subtree remains available under
the MIT License.

Per Apache-2.0 section 4(b), that code has been modified. Substantive changes
made in AgentController include:

  * Security hardening — the gesture viewer binds loopback only, and child
    processes are spawned with argument arrays instead of interpolated shell
    strings.
  * Reliability — deadlines on WebDriverAgent requests, timeouts on every
    simctl invocation, and parallelised device discovery.
  * Payload efficiency — compact JSON tool output and downscaled JPEG
    screenshot previews (roughly 4x smaller responses).
  * Surface expansion — the tool catalog grew from 10 tools to 36, with MCP
    annotations on every tool.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Original to AgentController
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The following are original work, not derived from either project above:

  * The macOS backend (Swift / AXUIElement / CGEvent / ScreenCaptureKit),
    including background-first automation, in which tools drive an application
    without stealing the user's focus, cursor, or frontmost window.
  * The Windows backend (C#/.NET / UI Automation / Win32 SendInput), including
    the foreground-authorization model for raw-input gestures.
  * The unified cross-platform tool contract that lets a single MCP client
    drive macOS, Windows, and iOS through one shared vocabulary.

